            <mxPoint x="490" y="3030" as="sourcePoint" />
            <mxPoint x="540" y="2980" as="targetPoint" />
          </mxGeometry>
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="QnWHKXO68kZ6Bdr08-0n-18" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;b style=&quot;background-color: transparent; color: light-dark(rgb(0, 0, 0), rgb(255, 255, 255));&quot;&gt;MCP:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;Standard,all most 1100, external system can be integrated, don&#39;t need to write schema and function calling (USB Port)&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Standare Protocol , to connect to external resources , just start MCP server and pass the details of external resource e.g. GitHub&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;In frontend you can connect to any external resource ( reduced&amp;nbsp; API calling&amp;nbsp;points)&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Now our service will call API, not human, MCP is wrapper over around 1100 external resources, machine will interact through MCP&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;div&gt;The MCP is an open standard developed by Anthropic to streamline how AI systems particularly LLM, connect and interact with external data sources and tools .Its designed to solve a key limitation of AI midels their isolation from real time ,dynamic data , instead of relying solely on static training data or requiring custom integration for every new data resource , MCP provides a universal sandardized way for AO applications to access and use information from various systems -like databases, APIs, file system or business tools-securily and efficiently.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;Think of MCP as a &quot;USB-C&quot; for AI Integrations just as a USB-C port allows different devices to connect to a computer using one standard , MCP enables AI models to plug into diverse data sources and tools through a single protocol .This reduce the complexity of building and maintaining seperate connections ,making AI systems more flexible ,scalable and context-aware.For example an AI assistannt using MCP could chaeck your calander,fetch file from google drive ,or query a database,all without bespoke code for each task&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;MCP Operates on a client-server architecture&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;MCP Hosts; These are AI applications (like a chatbot or an IDE plugin) that need access to external data or capabilities&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;MCP Clients: These sit within the host and manage secure ,one-to one connections to the servers&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;MCP Servers- These are lightweight programs that expose specific tools,data,or resources (e.g. GitHub Server might provide repository access) to AI.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;div&gt;The protocol uses JSON-RPC 2.0 for communications,allowing dynamic, two-way interactions-such as fetching real-time data or execurting actions-while incorporating security features like access controls .It also supports dynamic tool discovery ,meaning the AI can configure out what tools or data available without hard-coded knowledge.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;In practice, MCP empowers AI to be more than just a text generator- It can act as an agent that interacts with world.Developer benifits from reduced integration overhead,and the open-source nature of MCP foster a growing ecosystem or reusable servers for platforms like slack,GitHub,or even local file systems,its a step forward making AI more practical and connected in real-world applications&lt;/div&gt;" style="text;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=0;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="3170" width="750" height="30" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
